d but perhaps even more  importantly adjust to changing global  environment will probably have a  profound impact and effect on the United  States and its role as a world power in  by the 50th year of this still very  young century this profound change will  affect the individual fortunes of and  aspirations of nearly everyone certainly  everyone in this room today especially  the younger members of the audience I'm  sorry to start off with what some might  think as a pessimistic tone but let me  add that by by nature I am an optimist  and I don't buy into some of the things  I hear talked about not only the United  States but elsewhere about doomsday  scenarios associated with regard to any  sort of the climb  in for the United States this country is  proven time and again that we are  resilient especially in the face of  adversity and especially in the face of  complex problems but i do believe as i  as i mentioned that we are at a critical  intersection in our history and that if  we are to take the right path at this  intersection there must be a clear  recognition of the challenges we face we  also need a clearer understanding that  the world we had so much to do with  creating during the latter half of the  20th century is changing faster than  anyone can imagine with very serious  implications that must be addressed but  my experience over a relatively long  career that the definition of change is  what we easily do to someone else it is  not easy to change what we do in this  individuals or as institutions or as  countries but we can't compete and we  can't be successful without implementing  change as environment changes and this  is true of individuals it's true of  businesses and it's true of Nations well  we know about the new environment that  we find ourselves in in the still-young  century is that the world is smaller  information travels faster the gap  between the haves and the have-nots is  increasing in the world that speed is  important in decisi