utting taxes and regulations  pushing legions of young people through  science technology and engineering  schools and building brand new  infrastructure in a fraction of the time  it takes to build anything here unquote  in my view when a nation stops being  able to do the things that are in it's a  known own interests the signs of decline  cannot be very far behind trend lines  for our competitive positions in our own  hemisphere in Africa for example or not  encouraging the world is catching up and  there's nothing wrong with that but  there is still time to remedy the  situation but not nearly the time the  amount of time that some people think  there is it's interesting to note at  least from my perspective how little of  our current political discourse in  either party one year before national  elections is being accorded to the issue  of American competitiveness in the world  I know that it must sound strange for  someone who spent 40 years in uniform to  stand before you declaring that American  leadership in the economic sector of our  society is the biggest threat to our  position in the world during the 21st  century but I I believe that this is so  matter of fact I heard a line the other  day from a friend of mine in California  who said the economy in California is so  bad even the earthquakes are moving east  on the negative side and I fully  understand that my preceding assertions  were not exactly uplifting the ordered  world composed of two principal  adversaries has largely disappeared but  what has replaced it is not a  particularly good thing either we do  face a long term conflict that is best  described as a war of ideologies in  which throughout much of the  underdeveloped where we are portrayed as  the principal reason for oppression  either by our physical presence on the  planet or by our support to forms of  government that are not that are  considered oppressive by the people the  so-called Arab Spring phenomenon  represents just how complex and  difficult  things are on the global playing field  today the impact of these global changes  to our role in the world of the 21st  century are profound in the first place  I am I'm unsure that the body politic  has grasped the significance of the  difference between the two centuries to  be sure from a national security  perspective we have made some very  important changes that have contributed  to our collective safety and security by  combining the National Security Council  and the Homeland Security Council's we  have recognized that there is no border  where international and national threats  are concerned in other words you can't  delineate we're one stop starts and the  other one stops we created a flatter  National Security Council that is better  organized to address not only the  geographical challenges of different  parts of the world but also the  functional challenges of things like  cyber security economic development  development the linkages between space  Global Development terror and  conventional threats are integrated in  the National Security Council of today  and highly visible on a daily basis the  National Security Council itself across  the four layers of decision-making all  the way up to the full national security  council which is headed of course by the  president has been expanded to reflect  the fact that most of our governments  departments and agencies have to be at  the table when they need to be in other  words the smaller group of claimants to  the national security portfolio Defense  Department National Security Council and  a little bit of the stapler now have  have been expanded to include in  national security things like commerce  and trade and economic policies and  cybersecurity the war on terror and the  like it's the operating principle  beginning in january of 2009 that i use  to encourage the heads of different  departments and agencies to be at the  table in the Situation Room when issues  that they were concerned with were being  discussed the operating principle was if  you're not at the table you're probably  on the menu and so you'd better be there  nonetheless much more remains to be done  in terms of making the United States  more capable not only competing but  leading in the 21st century and this is  what it's all about one of the critical  shortfalls of our national security  portfolio for example is the continued  absence of any semblance of an energy  policy for the United States a condition  that has existed for over 40 years  surely this is a cross-cutting national  security issue of the first order at  best we have an a la carte policy when  from time to time the proponents have a  certain sector of our energy portfolio  broadcast and fund their opinions boone  Pickens comes to mind talking about gas  and wind and solar power nothing wrong  with that but the fact that we do not  publish a national energy strategy that  Americans can read and understand where  we're going with regard to the use of  energy and how we use it and what the  potential of the future is for our  energy needs is not only a national  failure it's an international failure  because other countries look to the  United States to lead in these sectors  and failure to lead only serves to drive  home the fact that the leadership that  we bring to the table is lacking this  may be an area where the private sector  will actually lead the public sector and  I believe that that at least for the  foreseeable future the paralysis that we  in the public sector and in the  discourses and debates and what it is  we're talking about is going to give way  to private sector leadership in order to  solve some of these problems to return  to the Arab Spring for a moment it's  important to realize that the the  traditional response package of the 20th  century which was usually purely  military in different parts of the world  is probably not what will be needed in  each and every crisis will face in the