nds and  allies the world over do not want to  contemplate a world in which United  States place a diminished role they want  the United States to be the leader that  inspires those who are struggling or  suffering and who absent that leadership  would have no counter balance to the  world of the autocratic and oppressive  regimes so for our young people this is  your calling this is your mission and  this is your responsibility standing on  the sidelines is not acceptable you must  not only understand the severity of the  problem we face in a new and more  competitive global environment in a  still new century but also that you must  be part of the solution  yesterday I was in Johnstown  Pennsylvania at the request of the  family of the late of Congressman John  Murtha who died unexpectedly about a  year ago and the citizens of his  district 12 District of Pennsylvania had  erected a statue in his memory in doing  some preparatory work on what I might  say about the life of Congressman Martha  who not only rose to great power in the  house of representative is one of the  most senior chairman over a 36-year  career but he was also a soldier  statesman and that he also achieved the  rank of colonel in the United States  Marine Corps Reserve and participated  tried to volunteer during Korea was not  sent to Korea but served on active duty  in Vietnam and and distinguished himself  I think many times by being wherever the  troops were so he could better  understand the nature of the conflicts  be they be they in Vietnam or  Afghanistan or Iraq but I was struck by  a they saying that his great grandmother  told him when he was a young man and  that saying is now carved at in the in  stone and on the statue that we unveiled  yesterday in Johnstown and his mother  his great grandmother said to him you  are we are put on this earth to make a  difference we are put on this earth to  make a difference and we should remember  that we are free as individuals and as a  nation 