utting taxes and regulations  pushing legions of young people through  science technology and engineering  schools and building brand new  infrastructure in a fraction of the time  it takes to build anything here unquote  in my view when a nation stops being  able to do the things that are in it's a  known own interests the signs of decline  cannot be very far behind trend lines  for our competitive positions in our own  hemisphere in Africa for example or not  encouraging the world is catching up and  there's nothing wrong with that but  there is still time to remedy the  situation but not nearly the time the  amount of time that some people think  there is it's interesting to note at  least from my perspective how little of  our current political discourse in  either party one year before national  elections is being accorded to the issue  of American competitiveness in the world  I know that it must sound strange for  someone who spent 40 years in uniform to  stand before you declaring that American  leadership in the economic sector of our  society is the biggest threat to our  position in the world during the 21st  century but I I believe that this is so  matter of fact I heard a line the other  day from a friend of mine in California  who said the economy in California is so  bad even the earthquakes are moving east  on the negative side and I fully  understand that my preceding assertions  were not exactly uplifting the ordered  world composed of two principal  adversaries has largely disappeared but  what has replaced it is not a  particularly good thing either we do  face a long term conflict that is best  described as a war of ideologies in  which throughout much of the  underdeveloped where we are portrayed as  the principal reason for oppression  either by our physical presence on the  planet or by our support to forms of  government that are not that are  considered oppressive by the people the  so-called Arab Spring phenomenon  represents just how complex and  difficult  things are on the global playing field  today the impact of these global changes  to our role in the world of the 21st  century are profound in the first place  I am I'm unsure that the body politic  has grasped the significance of the  difference between the two centuries to  be sure from a national security  perspective we have made some very  important changes that have contributed  to our collective safety and security by  combining the National Security Council  and the Homeland Security Council's we  have recognized that there is no border  where international and national threats  are concerned in other words you can't  delineate we're one stop starts and the  other one stops we created a flatter  National Security Council that is better  organized to address not only the  geographical challenges of different  parts of the world but also the  functional challenges of things like  cyber security economic development  development the linkages between space  Global Development terror and  conventional threats are integrated in  the National Security Council of today  and highly visible on a daily basis the  National Security Council itself across  the four layers of decision-making all  the way up to the full national security  council which is headed of course by the  president has been expanded to reflect  the fact that most of our governments  departments and agencies have to be at  the table when they need to be in other  words the smaller group of claimants to  the national security portfolio Defense  Department National Security Council and  a little bit of the stapler now have  have been expanded to include in  national security things like commerce  and trade and economic policies and  cybersecurity the war on terror and the  like it's the operating principle  beginning in january of 2009 that i use  to encourage the heads of different  departments and agencies to be at the  table in the Situation Room when issues  that they were concerned with were being  discussed the operating principle was if  you're not at the table you're probably  on the menu and so you'd better be there  nonetheless much more remains to be done  in terms of making the United States  more capable not only competing but  leading in the 21st century and this is  what it's all about one of the critical  shortfalls of our national security  portfolio for example is the continued  absence of any semblance of an energy  policy for the United States a condition  that has existed for over 40 years  surely this is a cross-cutting national  security issue of the first order at  best we have an a la carte policy when  from time to time the proponents have a  certain sector of our energy portfolio  broadcast and fund their opinions boone  Pickens comes to mind talking about gas  and wind and solar power nothing wrong  with that but the fact that we do not  publish a national energy strategy that  Americans can read and understand where  we're going with regard to the use of  energy and how we use it and what the  potential of the future is for our  energy needs is not only a national  failure it's an international failure  because other countries look to the  United States to lead in these sectors  and failure to lead only serves to drive  home the fact that the leadership that  we bring to the table is lacking this  may be an area where the private sector  will actually lead the public sector and  I believe that that at least for the  foreseeable future the paralysis that we  in the public sector and in the  discourses and debates and what it is  we're talking about is going to give way  to private sector leadership in order to  solve some of these problems to return  to the Arab Spring for a moment it's  important to realize that the the  traditional response package of the 20th  century which was usually purely  military in different parts of the world  is probably not what will be needed in  each and every crisis will face in the  21st century while each problem will be  different will have different  characteristics each will require at  least some diagnosis that includes some  thinking with regard to economic  solutions security of course and  governance these are remedies that will  most likely be applied by international  coalition's unilateral solutions such as  the what we used to do in the 20th  century could most likely have a  negative outcome if not applied with  great care and sensitivity we've take  the case of Egypt and this is a  developing issue that no one can really  predict how how it will turn out but  it's one that's extremely strategic and  very very important that it turned out  the right way in the early days of the  of the upheaval in Egypt the army played  a very critical role in in managing the  crisis in a way that resulted in many  people not being killed and hurt  unnecessarily the army advocated a  willingness to shift to to listen to the  people who were rising up and have a  lead Egypt towards more democratic ways  they immediately proclaimed election  dates which would have been postponed  several times  but the problem there if you look at it  to illustrate my point is that there was  no need to send in any US troops but  there is a great need for economic  development in in order to give the  people of Egypt a belief that not only  the United States but other developed  countries would would like to help them  solve these problems so there is a great  need for assistance with regard to  governance and rule of law stamping out  corruption in short the portfolio of our  response packages in the 21st century  will be different and it will have to  include those kinds of things and we're  struggling with that in terms of how to  reform our government because most of  the resources are invested in the  Defense Department some in the State  Department but to have a comprehensive  package that is required requires that  other agencies and are in our government  also have adequate resources in order to  participate one seems to me one need  only look at their frustrations created  by Afghanistan Pakistan and Iraq to see  that the future of each of those nations  may well be very different than what we  had intended at the onset of this decade  it's not to suggest that us engagement  is obsolete or even worse unneeded it is  most assuredly not in fact I think us  engagement is more important than ever  but it's the way we engage that will  have to be adjusted and the reason for  that is the sovereign governments have  sensitivities that are at an all-time  high and no one wants the scene wants to  be seen as block buckling under to the  US or any other country for that matter  and the other thing that's changed on  the global landscape is that as a result  of the pure competitors that we face  nations have alternatives that in  they didn't have just 15 years ago and  they use those alternatives very  skillfully playing playing us off  against others and others off against us  in order to do what's in their national  interest so the so-called American  Century of the 20th century is not  necessarily over there is no magic here  in terms of when it starts and what it  ends but the new century will be  different this is the world that our  young people will live in and compete in  we can't ignore it we cannot withdraw  from it as some would have you believe  and we cannot fail unless we're willing  to accept a lesser role for this nation  I am convinced that Americans still  expect that at the end of the day the  United States will lead the world in  most arenas not just the military domain  that was what led the Soviet that's led  to the Soviet Union's demise Soviet  Union was a superpower in one area only  and it wasn't enough and it is not  enough for the United States so to leave  you with an encouraging thought I will  tell you that after 65 years of  traveling the world over I'm convinced  more than ever that are many friends and  allies the world over do not want to  contemplate a world in which United  States place a diminished role they want  the United States to be the leader that  inspires those who are struggling or  suffering and who absent that leadership  would have no counter balance to the  world of the autocratic and oppressive  regimes so for our young people this is  your calling this is your mission and  this is your responsibility standing on  the sidelines is not acceptable you must  not only understand the severity of the  problem we face in a new and more  competitive global environment in a  still new century but also that you must  be part of the solution  yesterday I was in Johnstown  Pennsylvania at the request of the  family of the late of Congressman John  Murtha who died unexpectedly about a  year ago and the citizens of his  district 12 District of Pennsylvania had  erected a statue in his memory in doing  some preparatory work on what I might  say about the life of Congressman Martha  who not only rose to great power in the  house of representative is one of the  most senior chairman over a 36-year  career but he was also a soldier  statesman and that he also achieved the  rank of colonel in the United States  Marine Corps Reserve and participated  tried to volunteer during Korea was not  sent to Korea but served on active duty  in Vietnam and and distinguished himself  I think many times by being wherever the  troops were so he could better  understand the nature of the conflicts  be they be they in Vietnam or  Afghanistan or Iraq but I was struck by  a they saying that his great grandmother  told him when he was a young man and  that saying is now carved at in the in  stone and on the statue that we unveiled  yesterday in Johnstown and his mother  his great grandmother said to him you  are we are put on this earth to make a  difference we are put on this earth to  make a difference and we should remember  that we are free as individuals and as a  nation thanks to the collective  sacrifice of many who came before us and  will continue to be free as a result of  the next greatest generation you young  people here are that generation and I  predict that you will be amazingly  successful thank you very much for  listening to my remarks
 

